Fix a self-referential `toolchains'` knot that hangs `v2-haddock`.

`CmdHaddock.haddockAction` rebuilds the `Staged Toolchain` so that the
Host toolchain carries the reconfigured haddock program db.  Both
branches of that rebuild referred to `toolchains'` — the value being
defined — instead of `toolchains`, the original:

    let toolchains' = Staged $ \case
          Host -> (getStage toolchains' Host){toolchainProgramDb = progs}
          Build -> getStage toolchains' Build

Forcing either stage therefore re-enters its own thunk.  The thread
blocks on its own blackhole and never makes progress: 0% CPU, no
syscalls, no child processes, and — because lazy blackholing in the
threaded RTS does not reliably turn a self-referential blackhole into
`<<loop>>` — no exception either.  `cabal v2-haddock` simply stops,
forever, and the build step has to be killed.

It only bites when something actually forces a stage's toolchain, which
is why it looks intermittent: a `v2-haddock` run with nothing to do
returns in milliseconds, while one that has to configure a unit (e.g.
because the `--haddock-*` flags shift the unit id, as they do for every
doc-enabled slice) wedges in the Configure phase, right after cabal
prints "Using in-library setup method with build-type Simple".

Use `toolchains` on the right-hand side, which is what was meant.

diff --git a/src/Distribution/Client/CmdHaddock.hs b/src/Distribution/Client/CmdHaddock.hs
index d741211..3706a1d 100644
--- a/src/Distribution/Client/CmdHaddock.hs
+++ b/src/Distribution/Client/CmdHaddock.hs
@@ -217,8 +217,8 @@ haddockAction relFlags targetStrings globalFlags = do
       $ getStage toolchains Host
 
   let toolchains' = Staged $ \case
-        Host -> (getStage toolchains' Host){toolchainProgramDb = progs}
-        Build -> getStage toolchains' Build
+        Host -> (getStage toolchains Host){toolchainProgramDb = progs}
+        Build -> getStage toolchains Build
 
   let buildCtx' =
         buildCtx
